for those of you who just want to get on  with the tips they're time stamped below  but more than just saving money i really  wanted to first explain why i thought it  was important to make this video so that  you can know your motivation as you go  through this list recently i've gotten  into the habit of writing down every  single one of our expenses to try to  figure out where we're spending the most  in and what we can cut back on oddly  enough i was surprised when i shared  with my husband that the category we  spent the least amount of money in is  our kids i was reading through the  comments on avery's channel talking  about how he saves 80 of his income and  i noticed a lot of comments stating  things like it's not achievable for  people with families and it's not  possible if you have kids so i tried to  be a little bit of an encouragement and  state that it certainly is possible by  giving a few quick tips and i got this  response  until they become teenagers and know  they're poor i'm not quite sure how to  take it i'm guessing it means that you  can give your kids a simple lifestyle  while they're young but then they'll  grow up and then they'll realize that  they're poor to which i lovingly respond  that both my husband and i grew up poor  my husband was worse off than i i  remember at one point in my life having  to share one meal with our family  and my mom's saying  i'm not hungry now i realize she was  hungry but she wouldn't eat to give me  her portion  my husband actually had days where he  had absolutely no food and he got a belt  whooping if he dared ask for food  because there was none he left the home  at 16 to be able to provide for the  family and slept on trees on more than  one occasion  fast forward many years we now live in  the usa and we are so blessed to never  have to worry about having food on the  table and yes our kids toys are very  minimal our wardrobes are as well you'll  see me repeating the same outfit on  several occasions but by no means would  i classify that as poor the question  that we asked ourselves is this do we  want our kids to base their happiness on  material possessions  or the relationships and memories that  they create  we choose the later of the two and this  is the entire reason why we choose and  love a minimalist lifestyle is because  it allows us to live the life that we  want a life where our focus is not on  acquiring more and a feeling of  emptiness as we realize there's always  something that we need or that we lack  but rather a feeling of freedom as we  let go of the excess and we find  ourselves perfectly content with  simplicity  some of the things on this list may seem  a little too extreme for some people and  that's okay we know our why so as we  continue on with this list keep in mind  that the things that you choose to do or  not do will depend on your why for doing  it you may choose to do only a few on  these list and that's perfectly okay as  long as it provides value for your  family so i'm really not trying to tell  you to do all of these things i'm just  trying to encourage and motivate you by  showing you some ideas that maybe you  hadn't thought of also i recognize that  some of you may have older kids so the  baby stuff will be towards the end of  the video okay all of that being said if  you like this video please give it a  thumbs up because it really helps out my  channel but if not then by all means  thumbs it down but please give me some  feedback in the comments so i know what  to work on number one buy all black  socks when our first was walking and  constantly pulling socks off or losing  them or leaving the white ones looking a  bit yucky we decided to simplify our  life by buying a pack of all black socks  this saves us from having to look for  lost loners when she outgrows them we  can pass them on to her son she does  have a couple of white ruffled socks for  dresses but otherwise this just makes  things easier and no more buying socks  number two always pack snacks and drinks  when going somewhere even if you think  it's just going to be a short trip just  in case it takes a little longer than  planned for example when my husband and  i take detours and get lost at the toy  store home depot and then we end up  buying whatever is at the checkout line  because we didn't bring a snack or a  drink  number three don't buy special event  clothes or holiday clothes unless you're  planning like a special family photo or  something kids really don't need a new  outfit for every holiday  number four sell your kids gently use  clothes if you did buy the special  holiday outfit or your daughter some  expensive carhartt overalls that she  only wore once  you know who you are  then you can sell your kids gently use  clothes to either other people online or  consignment or thrift shops number five  buy used clothing this goes without  saying kids outgrow their clothing so  quickly so you can save tons of money  with this tip alone but if you don't  like the idea of your kids using used  clothes then there are actually also  consignment shelves that sell clearance  style items for stores at a discounted  price when we want to buy our kids new  shoes for example we shop at a  consignment store that sells clearanced  out target items  for less than half the price so check  out stores that might be in your area  number six don't buy toys that your kids  will outgrow quickly or that won't last  buy toys for a wide age group for  example wooden blocks musical  instruments or animal figures  as an example we don't have a rattle for  our baby if he wants to he can have at  it with some maracas  or he can throw wooden blocks everywhere  keeping in mind that you keep the toys  safe of course like no small parts the  toys that your older kids use can work  just as well for your smaller kids for  more on the topic of toys i highly  recommend the parenting junkies video on  the 10 categories of toys that kids need  number seven once your kids have the  amount of toys that you want for them to  have don't buy them new toys instead  allow them to play with random objects  for example that cardboard box that you  were about to throw out or that empty  formula can that makes a great drum  i love to give my daughter random  objects because i use her i'd see her  use her creativity and also because it's  a new toy it sparks her curiosity and  then once she's done and she's bored  with it i can just toss it out like i  intended anyway sometimes the random  objects in the house are actually the  ones that they enjoy the most give me a  thumbs up emoji if you ever played with  random objects around the house and let  me know what it was  number eight buy one toy collection that  is meant for all your kids to share and  only have a few toys that are specific  for each child for example we purchased  several categories of toys like musical  instruments cooking set blocks and the  expectation is that these toys are to be  shared they do have a few toys that are  specific to them that are special for  them and these toys tend to be christmas  or birthday gifts from family  number nine buy used furniture like beds  lamps side tables or make your own  furniture if you can  this trundle bag was a hundred dollars  and really all it needed was some paint  my husband built a rail and voila we can  use the trundle for another bed or use  it as storage  number 10 avoid purchasing special  character items or prints and patterns  that they may quickly outgrow this can  mean princess blankets superhero book  bags unicorn cups lamps you name it once  they've outgrown this or they're moved  on to the next special character that  they like you're out having to purchase  all this stuff all over again instead  buy something that's simple but yet  tasteful that they can use for years to  come also this can teach them that they  don't always need to have the latest  trend and can help them instill in them  from the very beginning that consumerism  is not what will lead them to happiness  number 11 child care for a lot of  parents this is easily the category they  spent the most amount of money in if you  can try finding somebody that you know  or a family member if willing and able  to take care of your kids  and for some parents and some families  it actually works out better if one  parent stays home versus if both were  working just because of where they fall  in the tax bracket and the kinds of  financial assistance available to them  that wouldn't be if both parents were  working so explore your options and be  creative with this and also if you have  any recommendations on how to save on  this please share them in the comments  because this is easily one of the  categories that parents spend the most  on number 12 feed them what you eat if  you can try to avoid buying the  prepackaged foods and snacks for your  kids and have them eat the same things  that you do if you have a baby i highly  recommend checking out baby lead weaning  and seeing if it's right for you and  your baby number 13 don't get them used  to buying snacks or novelty items every  time you go to a store when i was a kid  i did not dare ask for something at the  checkout line because i knew what the  answer would be now if you want to do  that every now and then go ahead but  don't make it to where they expect it  every time  this will quickly drain your wallet  number 14 size up their winter jackets  let's face it puffy jackets are meant to  be big anyway so if you size them up  they will not only be able to layer them  for that winter but they can also use  them for the next season and this can  actually be done with boots and tennis  shoes in the winter by wearing them with  an extra sock i actually used to love  this when i was a child because my feet  were always warm number 15 use your  library you don't have to own every book  or movie and also check out if your  library has an app or an e-version to  where you can download books online we  use the hoopla app and we use it to  check out books but i have also seen  kids shows and movies as well and some  libraries also hold events where they  have  like little parties and things for kids  as well so check it out  number 16 for birthday and christmas  gifts ask that friends and family don't  give new toys and instead ask for what  they need for example craft supplies  that they may go through quickly  for christmas last year my adoptive mom  asked what our daughter needed and we  said that we wanted to build a farm  table  and she kindly gave us the money to buy  the material to make it this of course  is more special because daddy made it  but also she knows that it was possible  because of grandma  number 17 another option if your kids  are older ask them and see if they would  be willing to ask friends and family  that instead of giving gifts  to give to a charity for example like  saint jude's this will not really save  money however it can help shift their  mindset by focusing on giving and what  they can do for their community instead  of me me me i don't remember ever  receiving birthday or christmas gifts  from my parents when i was young but i  honestly wouldn't change this about my  past because i learned not to expect it  and my hope is that with our kids that  we can teach them to think instead about  what can i give what can i do what do i  have to offer at least that's the hope  anyway we'll see number 18 moving on to  baby stuff skip the bassinets the cribs  the toddler beds and go straight to a  floor bed  of course this goes without saying  always ensure safety first when we did  this with our daughter we had to make  sure that for example furniture wouldn't  fall on her there weren't toys that she  was going to be able to grab that were  going to  be a choking hazard so always ensure  safety but we loved this for many  reasons  of course it easily saved us cash by not  buying all those other items but when  she wasn't able to walk yet she could  crawl in and out of the bed on her own  it kind of gave her a little bit more of  independence also if it was a  particularly rough night i could just  crawl in the bed right with her and she  never had to get used to her big girl  bed because she was always in it  bassinets and cribs are only used for a  short time anyway and then you still  have to go and buy a bed so you can skip  that all together and just get it from  the very beginning also there's no worry  that she's gonna fall on the floor  because it's already on the floor number  19 don't buy baby shoes it was hard to  convince my husband that buying our kids  baby shoes was a waste of time and money  i mean they don't walk so they don't  need them and half the time you're  running around looking for the darn  things because they can't seem to stay  on their feet plus i think if i were a  baby i would want my feet to be comfy  not cute  number 20 for newborns and infants buy  as much gender-neutral as you can for  example clothes swaddles carriers baby  bottles all that sort of stuff if this  is your first child especially and you  plan to have more children kids outgrow  their clothes so quickly when they're  babies and so you have to buy a new  wardrobe every few months this easily  saved us a lot of money with our son but  it's also the case for big ticket items  like strollers and car seats get stuff  that can be passed down this can easily  save you hundreds  number 21 it may not be for everyone but  buy reusable diapers wipes and training  underpants when our daughter was born we  invested about five hundred dollars in  diapers and it has easily saved us  thousands now that she's potty trained  and her brother is not using them we  chose to buy ours new so it was a little  bit more pricey but you can buy them  online there's lots of parents trying to  sell their used diapers all the time so  if you're okay with that and you want  extra savings maybe check that out as an  option number 22 don't buy baby mittens  they fall off half the time anyway if  your baby is especially a houdini and  seems to scratch use socks instead i'm  telling you they look funny but they  work odd daughter especially had a tough  time with maxima and she would scratch  and bleed and socks were the only thing  that worked it may look funny but it  worked number 23 skip the transitional  eating utensils this tip is montessori  inspired but with our first we did not  buy the baby spoons the cups with the  handles on them or the sippy lids  instead what we did was we bought these  munchkin stainless steel cups that have  a sippy lid and a straw lid attachment  and i think she actually likes this a  lot better because our cups are  stainless steel so it makes her feel  like a grown-up and they can be used for  years to come  number 24 if your insurance offers it  get a free breast pump and breastfeed if  you can breastfeeding easily saves you  cash it's best for baby and can help you  get your figure back a big money saver  for me has also been having reusable  breast milk bags so that i don't have to  be constantly buying the disposable bags  number 25 don't buy trendy baby gadgets  babies don't really need those pacifiers  with the stuffed animals on them gliders  and walkers with our first we had a  walker that had music and buttons on it  and she actually preferred to sit down  and play with the buttons instead of  actually walking with it  of course buttons are more entertaining  so that was 25 ways to save with kids  but for sticking to the end my bonus tip  for you is adopt minimalism in your life  if as a whole you start to remove excess  from your life and become happier with  less it will in turn rub off on them as  they see that there is joy to be had in  simplicity if you have any ideas on how  to save money with kids please share  them in the comments it may be difficult  and may require creativity but it is  definitely possible so let's encourage  each other  hasta luego

